I hope you're doing well. I wanted to follow up with a summary of our findings after reviewing the documents you submitted, including the purchase and sale agreement with William Lichtenstein and Laura Price (hereinafter “Seller”), the pictures, and materials related to the purchase of your property at 142 Annwood Road, Palm Harbor, Florida 34685.

As discussed, the primary concern is whether seller could be held liable for failing to disclose a prior flood in the property. You purchased the home in February 2024. When you purchased the property, you knew the property was in a flood zone as you had to purchase flood insurance. There is a creek in the back of the property, which was dry during the time of purchase. However, during the summer of 2024, the rains caused the creek to crest and property almost flooded. You called the realtor inquiring about prior floods, and you were advised that the Seller checked “ I Don’t Know” regarding flooding. Despite the Seller’s disclosure, you received a notice from the insurance company regarding a prior flood in the home in 2015.

Subsequently, the property flooded as a result of Hurricane Milton. You filed an insurance claim and had over $32,000.00 in damages. In addition, over the last year neighbors have advised that the home is a flood home. The insurance company did pay the claim covering all damages except the deductible. You would like to know your options concerning this matter and whether the Seller is liable for failing to disclose prior flooding in the property.

Here are the key points of our analysis:

• Florida law generally requires sellers to disclose known, material defects that are not readily observable. If the evidence of a prior flood were not visible during your visits, it may be considered a latent defect—triggering a duty to disclose if the seller had actual knowledge of the defect.
• Prior flooding qualifies as a material fact because it may indicate structural issues, mold risk, and could impact property value and insurability.
• To succeed on a claim against the seller for failure to disclose, you must present evidence that the seller had actual knowledge of the prior flooding prior to closing.
• Knowing you must purchase flood insurance is not equivalent to knowing the property previously flooded. Purchasing flood insurance is an insurance requirement and does not reveal the history flooding nor the extent of prior damage to the property.
• In addition to breach of a duty to disclose, you may have a cause of action for fraudulent concealment, which occurs when a seller intentionally makes a false statement concerning a material fact. This may be evidenced by the lack of seller’s disclosure and marking “I Don’t Know” on the disclosure form.
• The seller may argue that the new flood was caused a separate natural disaster that would have happened regardless of the flood history. Also, the damages from the hurricane are not related to the nondisclosure.
• A review of the public records show that the Seller purchased the property in 2022, giving the Seller a possible argument of no knowledge of the flood history, especially if they can provide evidence of that they flood history was not disclosed during their purchase.
• To prove diminution in value of the property as a result of the prior flood would require extensive evidence, especially since the flood occurred almost a decade before the sale and the market conditions may have changed significantly. Due to issues proving diminution in value, the damages as a result of the failure to disclose may be limited to the deductible paid for the insurance claim.

Based on the above, a negotiated resolution would likely be your most efficient and cost-effective path forward. One option would be to issue a demand letter to the seller requesting reimbursement of the insurance claim deductible. This may lead to a joint resolution that includes mutual waivers and a clear release of future liability. However, please note, that the seller could reject the demand, especially if you do not have evidence of the seller having actual knowledge of the issues.

